---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **「what country made this?」에 답하는 자리.** (`/is-it-korean`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2026-08-29) ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * Search Console 실측에 제작국을 묻는 질의가 있다 — `what country made solo leveling` 22위,
 * `what country is solo leveling in` 36위. 노출은 작지만 **아무도 답을 안 하고 있다.**
 *
 * ── ⭐ 그런데 이 지면의 «진짜» 값어치는 따로 있다 ─────────────
 * 우리 도구는 974편마다 「이름만으로 한국 작품이라 가릴 수 있나」를 이미 재 두었는데
 * (koreaOnly 525 · shared 423 · unknown 15 · koreaUnconfirmed 11)
 * 🔴 **그 판정이 지금까지 어느 지면에도 안 실렸다.** 손님은 974편이 다 한국 것인 줄 안다.
 *
 * ⭐ 이 지면은 **우리 수가 틀렸을 수 있는 자리를 우리 손으로 내놓는다.**
 *   Wikidata 에 한국이 «아예 없는» 것이 41편 — That Night 은 115자리를 차지했다.
 *   「재 보고 안 된다고 적는 것도 결과다」를 지면으로 만든 것이다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 「shared」를 「틀렸다」로 쓰지 않는다 — **못 가른다**는 뜻이다.
 * ⛔ 못 가른 작품을 표에서 몰래 빼지 않는다. 이름을 적고 왜인지 적는다.
 * ⛔ Wikidata 의 country of origin 을 「제작국」이라 단정하지 않는다 —
 *   공동제작·권리사까지 들어가는 칸이다.
 *
 * ⛔ 화면에 한국어를 내지 않는다 — 이 주석은 frontmatter 라 나가지 않는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';
import data from '../../data/kcw-is-it-korean.json';

const c = data.counts;
const 몫 = (a: number) => Math.round((a / data.titleCount) * 100);

const TITLE = 'Is it actually Korean? For 423 of 974 Netflix titles, the name alone cannot tell you';
const DESC = 'Netflix publishes a title and a rank and nothing else — no year, no country, no '
  + 'director. So when The Glory, Business Proposal or Little Women appears on a chart, the name '
  + 'may belong to a Korean work or to another country’s. Here is every case, counted, including '
  + 'the 41 where our own figures may be wrong.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  variableMeasured: ['Country of origin agreement', 'Chart places'],
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/is-it-korean">
  <h1>Is it actually Korean?</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Netflix&rsquo;s weekly top 10 files give a title, a country, a week and a rank.
    <b>They do not give a year, a director, or a country of production.</b> So when a chart says
    &ldquo;Little Women&rdquo; or &ldquo;The Glory&rdquo;, the row itself cannot tell anyone which
    work that is &mdash; and every site counting &ldquo;Korean titles on Netflix&rdquo;, ours
    included, is making an inference at that point.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    We check each of our {data.titleCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} names against Wikidata: which
    countries have a film or series carrying exactly this English label?
    <b>{c.koreaOnly} names are carried only by Korean works</b> &mdash; those cannot be confused.
    <b>{c.shared} are carried by works from more than one country</b>, which is
    {' '}{몫(c.shared)}% of everything we publish figures for.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The four answers, counted</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What Wikidata says about the name</th><th class="num">Titles</th><th class="num">Share</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Only Korean works carry it</b> &mdash; cannot be confused</td>
          <td class="num">{c.koreaOnly}</td><td class="num">{몫(c.koreaOnly)}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Works from more than one country carry it</b> &mdash; the row alone cannot say which</td>
          <td class="num">{c.shared}</td><td class="num">{몫(c.shared)}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>No Korean work is listed under this name at all</b></td>
          <td class="num">{c.koreaUnconfirmed}</td><td class="num">{몫(c.koreaUnconfirmed)}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Wikidata has no usable entry</b> &mdash; unmeasured, not foreign</td>
          <td class="num">{c.unknown}</td><td class="num">{몫(c.unknown)}%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>Shared does not mean wrong.</b> Most shared names are almost certainly the Korean work
      &mdash; a Korean series charting across Asia in the week it aired is not going to be a
      1994 American film of the same name. Shared means the row on its own cannot prove it,
      and we would rather you knew which figures rest on an inference.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The biggest titles whose names are shared</h2>
    <p class="sub">Ranked by chart places. Countries listed are every country Wikidata attaches to a work of that exact name.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th class="num">Chart places</th><th>Also the name of a work from</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.sharedBiggest.map((x: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">{x.hasPage && x.slug ? <a href={`/title/${x.slug}`}>{x.title}</a> : x.title}</td>
            <td class="num">{x.places?.toLocaleString('en-US') ?? '—'}</td>
            <td class="fine">{x.countries.filter((y: string) => y !== 'South Korea').join(', ')}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The {data.notKoreaListedTotal} where our figures may be wrong</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      For these, Wikidata lists <b>no Korean work at all</b> under the name. Either Wikidata is
      incomplete, or we have counted another country&rsquo;s work as Korean. We publish the list
      rather than quietly dropping the rows, because dropping them would make our totals look
      cleaner than they are.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th class="num">Chart places</th><th>Wikidata lists</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.notKoreaListed.map((x: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">{x.hasPage && x.slug ? <a href={`/title/${x.slug}`}>{x.title}</a> : x.title}</td>
            <td class="num">{x.places?.toLocaleString('en-US') ?? '—'}</td>
            <td class="fine">{x.countries.join(', ')}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      If you know which work any of these rows belongs to, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> and it
      goes on <a href="/corrections">corrections</a> with the date. That is the whole mechanism:
      we are not able to resolve these from the data alone, and pretending otherwise would be the
      dishonest option.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which countries our titles most often share a name with</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th class="num">Of our {data.titleCount} names, how many it also carries</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.overlapCountries.map((x: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{x.country}</td>
            <td class="num">{x.titles}
              <span class="bar" style={`--w:${Math.round((x.titles / data.overlapCountries[0].titles) * 100)}%`}></span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      This says nothing about either country&rsquo;s output. English titles are short and reuse
      the same few hundred words, so the countries that make the most English-labelled work
      collide with everyone, Korea included.
    </p>
  </section>

  {data.unlabelled.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Titles we hold but have not checked yet</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        These have a Wikidata entry keyed to them but no country check completed. They are counted
        as unmeasured on this page, not as Korean and not as foreign.
      </p>
      <p class="fine">{data.unlabelled.map((x: any) => x.title).join(' &middot; ')}</p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section>
    <h2>What this page cannot answer</h2>
    <ul class="cant">
      {data.cannotAnswer.map((x: string) => <li>{x}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to walk from here</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title we hold</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/hit-or-flop">Where a title sits among all {data.titleCount}</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/netflix-which-country">Which countries charted a given title</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/provenance">Where every number on this site comes from</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/corrections">What we have got wrong and fixed</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/korean-movies-on-netflix">Korean films, counted separately</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/by-country">Every country, and how Korean its chart is</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwMethodLimits
    method={data.method + ` Chart places come from Netflix's own weekly top 10 files by country, ${data.weekFrom} to ${data.weekTo}, across ${data.marketCount} countries. A title is counted here once, however many countries and weeks it charted in.`}
    limitation={`Wikidata's country of origin field records co-producers and rights holders as well as the country a work was made in, so a country listed against a name is not a production credit and we do not present it as one. Wikidata is also incomplete and edited by volunteers, so a name it does not carry may still exist. Where Wikidata has no usable entry we count the title as unmeasured — never as foreign, never as Korean. None of this resolves which work actually charted: Netflix publishes a name and a rank and nothing that would settle it.`}
  />

  <style>
    .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .95rem; margin: .2rem 0 1rem; }
    .fine { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; }
    section { margin: 2.4rem 0; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .95rem; margin-bottom: .8rem; }
    th, td { padding: .45rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: left;
      vertical-align: baseline; }
    th { font-weight: 600; font-size: .82rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em;
      color: var(--muted); }
    .num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
    .nm { min-width: 10rem; }
    .bar { display: block; height: 3px; margin-top: .25rem; width: var(--w);
      background: var(--accent); opacity: .55; margin-left: auto; }
    .cant { color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
    .cant li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
    @media (max-width: 640px) { table { font-size: .88rem; } .nm { min-width: 7rem; } }
  </style>
</WikiTip>
